```dot
digraph KFoldTuning {
  rankdir=TB;
  graph [fontname="Arial", bgcolor="white", pad=0.2, nodesep=0.35, ranksep=0.55];
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Arial", fontsize=10,
        color="#536878", fontcolor="#17324D"];
  edge [color="#536878", fontname="Arial", fontsize=9, arrowsize=0.8];

  data [label="Training dataset", fillcolor="#FFE8A3"];
  configs [label="Candidate hyperparameter\nconfigurations", fillcolor="#FFD1DC"];
  folds [label="For each configuration:\nrun K-fold cross-validation", shape=oval,
         fillcolor="#CDEBFF"];

  subgraph cluster_folds {
    label="Example: K = 3";
    style="rounded,filled";
    color="#91C9B7";
    fillcolor="#EAF7F2";

    f1 [label="Fold 1 held out\nTrain on folds 2–3", fillcolor="#D9F2E6"];
    f2 [label="Fold 2 held out\nTrain on folds 1, 3", fillcolor="#D9F2E6"];
    f3 [label="Fold 3 held out\nTrain on folds 1–2", fillcolor="#D9F2E6"];

    f1 -> f2 [style=dashed, label="next fold"];
    f2 -> f3 [style=dashed, label="next fold"];
  }

  scores [label="Aggregate validation scores\n(e.g., mean across K folds)", fillcolor="#D9D2FF"];
  select [label="Select configuration\nwith best mean score", shape=diamond,
          fillcolor="#FFC99B"];
  refit [label="Refit selected model\non the full training dataset", fillcolor="#B8E0F2"];

  data -> configs;
  configs -> folds;
  folds -> f1;
  folds -> f2;
  folds -> f3;
  f1 -> scores;
  f2 -> scores;
  f3 -> scores;
  scores -> select;
  select -> refit;
}
```

Save the code as a `.dot` file and render it with, for example, `dot -Tpng kfold_tuning.dot -o kfold_tuning.png`.

### Evaluation

Assistant B is closer to the standard K-fold hyperparameter-tuning workflow: it shows folds, validation, aggregation of performance, and selection of hyperparameters. Its main omission is that it depicts only “Hyperparameter Set A,” rather than showing that multiple candidate configurations are each evaluated across the folds. It also does not show refitting the selected configuration on the full training dataset. These omissions make the tuning loop less explicit, but the explanation is broadly accurate.

Assistant A describes something closer to **nested cross-validation**, where an outer held-out fold is evaluated after hyperparameter selection inside the remaining data. That can be a valid approach if the inner search itself uses cross-validation, but its diagram does not show the inner folds needed to explain that process. It also has a confusing iteration path: `Score_k` loops back to the same `Fold_k`, while `Score_K` is never defined and points to a separate last-fold node. As a result, it is less clear and more misleading for the requested schematic.

Both responses provide colorful DOT code and rendering instructions. B is the more relevant and coherent answer, though it would benefit from explicitly showing multiple hyperparameter candidates and the final refit.

My final verdict is: [[B>A]]

